{
  "dom_id": "step-q96928",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "The position $r$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by $r(t)=⟨t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.$ Find the normal component of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t.$",
  "worked_solution": "The normal component of acceleration is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{N}=\\frac{||v\\times a||}{||v||}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $v\\times a,||v||$ and $||v\\times a||:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\times a & =⟨1,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =⟨(-{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t),-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ||v|| & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ ||v\\times a|| & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the normal component of the acceleration, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{N} & =\\frac{||v\\times a||}{||v||} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
